Objective: To characterize sex-dependent transcriptomic
changes in the dura speci ﬁc to sleep disruption-induced
migraine which represent potential therapeutic targets.
Background: Migraine is the second most common cause
of disability in the world and the most common cause of dis-
ability in reproductive-aged women. Sleep disruption is the
second most common trigger, but its pathophysiology and
sex differences in expression are not fully understood. We
previously developed and validated a sleep disruption-induced
migraine model using dim light which was used in this
study.
Design/Methods: We habituated 6 female and 6 male
C57BL/6J mice to 0.6g periorbital mechanical stimulation
