The International Classi ﬁcation of Headache Disorders
(ICHD) have been establishe d to identify headache types
and diagnose patients. The ICHD is based on expert opin-
ion and many other symptoms have been associated with
multiple headache subtypes (eg. light headedness) that are
not included in the ICHD-3, largely due to lack of signi ﬁ-
cant evidence for speci ﬁcity and sensitivity. We performed
an exploratory analysis of patient-reported headache intake
questionnaires in youth ages 6 to 17 years old presenting
with headache who reported PedMIDAS scores and overall
headache pain severity. We applied multiple correspondence
analysis (MCA) to symptoms associated with headache to
determine if there were underlying symptom clusters.
Importantly, MCA was agnostic to ICHD-3 diagnosis and
inclusion/exclusion criteria were purposefully broad to cap-
ture the fullest range of headache characteristics. The cohort
was likely biased more towards more severe headaches lead-
ing them to seek care. The cohort included 6,662 partici-
