S115. Dissecting the Causes and Contributors to Rapidly
Progressive Dementia: An Autopsy Study
Gregory S. Day, MD MSc MSCI , Evelyn B. Lazar, MD,
Nikhil B. Ghayal, BS, Shanu Roemer, MD, Neill R. Graff-
Radford, MD, Dennis Dickson, MD. Mayo Clinic, Jacksonville,
FL, USA.
Objective: To determine the clinical features and neuropath-
ological causes of rapidly progressive dementia (RPD) in a
neurodegenerative brain bank.
Background: Most series exploring the causes of RPD rely
on clinical diagnoses, with rare autopsy-con ﬁrmed diagnoses.
This approach is confounded by remarkable heterogeneity in
the clinical presentation of neurodegenerative diseases and
the potential for multiple distinct neuropathologies to mani-
fest in a single patient. A clearer understanding of the neuro-
pathological causes of RPD is needed to inform the diagnosis
and treatment of patients.
Methods: Patients with disease duration <4.0 years (symp-
tom onset to death due to dementia) were identi ﬁed within
