the Mayo Clinic neurodegenerative brain bank (1998-2020).
Available clinical records were reviewed, and relevant clinical
details extracted. Neuropathological diagnoses were assigned
following standard protocols.
Results: 306/8586 (3.6%) cases met RPD criteria, with
average disease duration of 2.9 /C61.0 years. Relative to typi-
cally progressive cases, prion disease most commonly pres-
ented as RPD (73%, 30/41), followed by progressive
supranuclear palsy/corticobasal degeneration (PSP/CBD:
7.4%, 141/1894), other frontotemporal lobar degeneration
(FTLD: 5.7%, 32/561), Lewy body disease (LBD: 4.1%,
49/1202), and Alzheimer disease (AD: 1.8%, 48/2687).
Most patients presented with cognitive complaints, except
patients with CBD/PSP in whom motor symptoms pre-
dominated. Patients with prion diseases had the shortest dis-
ease course (1.6 /C61.3 years; p<0.001). Coexisting pathologies
were frequent with two or more pathologies identi ﬁed in
43.5% of patients. The number of pathologies was not asso-
ciated with symptomatic duration. In a multivariable model,
increasing age ( β=-0.18 per decade, 95%CI: -0.28, -0.01)
and the presence of CJD ( β=-1.2, 95%CI: -1.6, -0.82) were
associated with shorter duration (years), while CBD/PSP
(β=0.29, 95%CI: 0.04, 0.54), AD ( β=0.41, 95%CI: 0.13,
0.70), and vascular disease ( β=0.26, 95%CI: 0.005, 0.52)
were associated with longer disease duration (adjusted
R
2=25%, p<0.001). Sex, APOEe4 carrier status, other
pathologies, and the presence of depression, hallucinations/
delusions, or sleep pathology were not associated with disease
duration.
Interpretation: Rapidly progressive forms of neurodegen-
erative diseases accounted for 3.6% of cases in this neurode-
generative brain bank. Symptomatic disease duration was not
explained by patient-speci ﬁc factors or coexisting pathology
suggesting that rapidly progressive neurodegenerative diseases
represent a distinct disease subgroup. The molecular charac-
teristics that contribute to rapidly progressive variants of neu-
rodegenerative disease warrant further consideration.
