S118. Evaluation of Skin Adhesion and Local Skin
Tolerability of Once-Weekly Donepezil Transdermal
System: Results of a Phase 1 Trial in Healthy Volunteers
Pierre N. Tariot, MD
1, Rene Braeckman, PhD 2, Charles Oh,
MD3. 1Banner Alzheimer ’s Institute, Phoenix, AZ, USA,
2KemPharm, Inc., Celebration, FL, USA, 3Corium Inc, Boston,
MA, USA.
Background: Oral donepezil, a reversible acetylcholinesterase
inhibitor, is the most used treatment for dementia of the
Alzheimer type and has been associated with gastrointestinal
distress, leading to treatment discontinuation. Transdermal
systems (TDSs) can provide an alternative mode of adminis-
tration that bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and offers sev-
eral other bene ﬁts over oral administration. Donepezil TDS
(Adlarity
®) was recently approved for the treatment of mild,
moderate, and severe dementia of the Alzheimer ’s type.
Methods: This was an open-label, randomized, 3-period,
crossover, phase 1 trial (NCT04617782) enrolling healthy volun-
teers aged 18-55 years. All participants received once-weekly
5-mg/d donepezil TDS during the5 - w e e kP e r i o d1 ,f o l l o w e db y
once-weekly 10-mg/d donepezil TDS or once-daily 10-mg oral
donepezil in the 5-week Period 2; treatments were switched in
Period 3. The primary endpoint was steady-state bioequivalence
of once-weekly donepezil TDS with once-daily oral donepezil.
Secondary endpoints included TDS adhesion to the skin, assessed
every 12 hours, during the weekly wear-periods of donepezil
TDSs. Skin irritation and tolerability assessments were performed
at 0.5, 24, 48, and 72 hours after TDS removal using the US
FDA-recommended dermal response scale and other effects scale.
Results: Of 568 TDSs applied (296 of 5-mg/d and 272 of
10-mg/d), 563 were included in the adhesion analysis. The
mean percentage (standard deviation) of TDS surface area
remaining adhered to the skin during weeks 1-5 was 92.6%
(10.9%) for 5-mg/d TDS and 93.3% (8.8%) for 10-mg/d
TDS. The number (%) of TDSs with ≥75% adhesion during
weeks 1-5 at all adhesion assessment timepoints during the
1-week wear period was 245 (83.6%) for 5-mg/d TDS and
232 (85.9%) for 10-mg/d TDS. Probability of a TDS
maintaining ≥75% adhesion throughout the wear period was
estimated as 0.8362 for 5-mg/d TDS and 0.8593 for 10-mg/d
TDS, and ≥80% of the TDSs were ≥75% adhered throughout
the 7-day wear period. Skin tolerability was similar for 5-mg/d
and 10-mg/d TDSs (combined skin irritation score of ≥3
reported in 51.7% and 54.5% of participants, 0.5 hours after
5- and 10-mg/d TDSs removal, respectively). No participant
discontinued because of skin irritation, and no TDSs were
removed because of unacceptable skin irritation.
Conclusion: Once-weekly 5-mg/d and 10-mg/d donepezil
TDSs demonstrated acceptable skin adhesion and skin tolera-
bility, supporting their use as treatment for dementia of the
Alzheimer type.
