Background: TDP-43 is a DNA/RNA-binding protein
whose aberrant aggregation is implicated in many neurode-
generative diseases, including Alzheimer ’s Disease (AD), and
normal aging. In AD, concomitant TDP-43 pathology
increases the likelihood of being cognitively impaired at
death. With our aging population, understanding the rela-
tionships between TDP-43, cognition, and age becomes
crucial.
Methods: Mayo Clinic has amassed a large database with
1072 participants with known TDP-43 pathology status and
AD spectrum diagnosis, who had been prospectively recruited
and followed in the NIH funded Mayo Clinic Alzheimer ’s
Disease Research Center, Mayo Clinic Study of Aging, and
Neurodegenerative Research Group. All underwent neuro-
logic, genetic, and neuropathologic evaluations and had data
