S121. Hemochromatosis Exacerbated Cognitive
Impairment
Rochelle M. Kinssies, OMS-IV
1, Nitin M. Butala, MD 2.
1Lake Erie College of Osteopathic Medicine, Bradenton, FL,
USA, 2Baptist Neurology Group, Jacksonville, FL, USA.
Iron accumulation in hemochromatosis has been demon-
strated to create a vast array of impairments and de ﬁcits,
including exacerbations of neurodegenerative diseases. It is
postulated the exacerbation may be caused by the increased
free radical formation derived from the Fenton reaction, cata-
lyzed by iron in its ferrous state. In this case study, a 54-year-
old Caucasian man with known genetic hemochromatosis,
treated with recurrent phlebotomies, in the setting of
unremarkable extensive blood work and brain imaging stud-
ies, presents with signi ﬁcant cognitive impairment noticed by
declining memory functions at work and at home, con ﬁrmed
by extensive neurocognitive testing. Global cognitive func-
tioning, estimated by an Overall Test Battery Mean
(OTBM), displayed mild-moderate impairment, suggestive of
decline when compared to his predicted baseline ability level.
His performance demonstrated signi ﬁcant cognitive slowing
for his age, with scores ranging from <1 percentile to 66th
percentile. His clinical presentation along with his test results
yields a diagnosis of mild neurocognitive disorder, possible
mixed etiology due to his hemochromatosis. This may be
consistent with very early signs of a neurodegenerative condi-
tion. Due to the decreased risk factors for early-onset cogni-
tive impairment, the etiology of his cognitive impairment
may be due to the iron accumulation in his brain from the
hereditary hemochromatosis. Hemochromatosis may be an
inciting or exacerbating factor in cognitive neurological
impairment, especially in a non-elderly patient. The develop-
ing link between hemochromatosis and cognitive impairment
and progression to AD may uncover novel treatment modali-
ties. Successfully treating the hemochromatosis may help
improve cognitive function secondary to neurodegenerative
exacerbation. Naturally, this exciting theory warrants further
study.
S123. Iatrogenic Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) Case:
Implications for Perioperative Risk Assessment and
Prevention
Claire Delpirou Nouh, MD , Maria Shoaib, MD, Jonathan
L. Garcia Ramiu, MD, Shawn Patel, MD, Juliane Chainakul,
APRN-CNS, David Lee Gordon, MD. The University of
Oklahoma Health Science Center, Oklahoma City, OK, USA.
Objectives: CJD is the most common human prion disease
and cause of a devastating rapidly progressive, fatal, cognitive
decline secondary to accumulation and propagation of
misfolded prion proteins. Iatrogenic CJD is rare, though cen-
tripetal spread of abnormal prion protein is described. Due to
a long preclinical course and delay in diagnosis, patients not
known to have CJD may undergo surgery with potential risk
of spread due to improper decontamination procedure in
between patients. We present a case of Heidenhain variant of
CJD (HvCJD) with a history of intraocular lens implants
two years prior to symptomatic disease and death.
Background: Most cases of CJD are sporadic or inherited.
Iatrogenic CJD is rare, most frequently occurring after sur-
gery involving the central nervous system, although other
exposures have been described including ophthalmologic pro-
cedures, in particularly corneal transplant, less likely after cat-
aract surgery. Early symptoms may be atypical, with visual
complaint or gait abnormality that can be attributed to other
common comorbidities and even lead to surgery. Classic
decontamination protocols are typically inadequate to prop-
erly eradicate prions from contaminated instruments, raising
concern of future exposure and potential transmission.
Case Report: 75-year-old woman s/p bilateral intraocular
lens implants two years earlier and no other potential CJD
risk factors was admitted for subacute, progressive dementia.
Two months prior to admission, she developed blurred vision
described as "seeing through Vaseline gel." A month prior to
admission, she developed progressively worsening memory
loss and apathy and, a week prior to admission, shuf ﬂing gait
and tremor with MoCA 27/30 that worsened to 15/30 on
the day of admission. MRI brain showed cortical ribboning
in the occipital, parietal, and temporal lobes bilaterally on
DWI and T2/FLAIR images. EEG showed continuous delta
slowing in the right frontotemporal region. CSF revealed pos-
itive 14.3.3 and elevated T-tau (1927 pg/ml), but negative
