S131. Prevalence of Stimulus Centered Neglect and
Viewer Centered Neglect in Left and Right Hemisphere
Stroke Patients
Ashley Raman, BS (2024) , Argye E. Hillis, MD. Johns
Hopkins University School of Medicine, Baltimore, MD, USA.
Patients with right hemisphere (RH) stroke are known to
experience left stimulus centered neglect (SCN) and left
viewer centered neglect (VCN). Less is known about the
prevalence of right SCN and VCN after left hemisphere
(LH) stroke. We analyzed performance on a gap-detection
test in 132 LH and 236 RH stroke patients at acute/subacute
(0-3 months) and chronic (6-12 months) stages. There were
no signi ﬁcant differences in sex, age, race, or education
between LH and RH stroke. They were instructed to circle
complete circles on the page and put an X on all incomplete
circles, which have gaps on the left or right sides. SCN is
observed when patients consistently circle incomplete circles
on the contralesional side and VCN is observed when
patients do not circle or put an X on any circles on the con-
tralesional side of the paper. We found that left SCN and left
VCN are equally prevalent in RH acute/subacute stroke. In
acute/subacute LH stroke, right SCN was signi ﬁcantly
(p=0.0082) more common than right VCN. SCN was
equally common in LH and RH stroke at the acute/subacute
stage, while RH patients were more likely to show left VCN
than LH patients to show right VCN (p=0.004). Finally, in
both RH and LH stroke, older patients (60+ years) were
more likely to make left SCN and VCN errors at the acute/
subacute time stage than younger patients (paired t-test:
p=0.0533), but age did not in ﬂuence right SCN or VCN
errors. Most patients improved by 6-12 months, when there
were no differences between LH and RH stroke.
