the context of acute ischemic stroke (AIS), but the degree
and distribution of impairment remains controversial. Here,
we evaluate the relationship between the degree of CA
impairment and both short and long-term clinical outcome.
Methods: Patients with stroke affecting a single cerebro-
vascular territory (ACA, MCA, or PCA) were eligible for
enrollment if symptoms onset was within 72 hours of proto-
col completion. Cerebral blood ﬂow velocity (CBFv) and
arterial blood pressure (ABP) were continuously monitored
using a TCD and a ﬁnger plethysmograph (Finapres
®
NOVA) respectively. 5 minutes of data were collected from
the ACA, MCA, and PCA in both hemispheres. A transfer
function analysis (TFA) quanti ﬁed the relationship between
ABP and CBFv in each vessel. Gain describes the magnitude
of CBF regulation, and phase describes the time-course of
autoregulation. Mx Index, also calculated for each vessel,
