Background: Intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH) survivors are
at high risk of neurological decline due to underlying progres-
sive cerebral small vessel disease (CSVD). Most ICHs are
attributable to two common forms of CSVD: cerebral amy-
loid angiopathy (CAA) and hypertensive-CSVD (HTN-
CSVD). HTN-CSVD includes patients with either pure deep
or mixed location ICH/microbleeds, the latter representing a
more severe form of HTN-CSVD. As hypertension control
represents the most potent intervention to delay CSVD pro-
gression after ICH, we investigated whether more severe
forms of HTN-CSVD are related to worse hypertension con-
trol in long-term follow-up after CSVD-related ICH.
Methods: We analyzed data from consecutive non-
traumatic ICH patients admitted between 2011 to 2020 to a
tertiary care center. Using CSVD markers derived from
MRIs, we classi ﬁed ICH patients as CAA-related (per modi-
ﬁed Boston criteria) and HTN-CSVD-related, further catego-
rizing deep and mixed location ICH/microbleeds within
HTN-CSVD. CSVD burden was quanti ﬁed using a validated
MRI-based score (range: 0-6 points). Longitudinal measure-
ments for systolic and diastolic BP following ICH were
obtained via semi-automated review of electronic health
records. We used linear mixed effects models to examine the
association of BP measurements during follow-up with
CSVD etiology and severity.
