Objective: To describe a case of an ischemic stroke as the
ﬁrst manifestation of Libman-Sacks endocarditis (LSE) in the
setting of antiphospholipid syndrome (APLS) in a patient
who was on apixaban for deep vein thrombosis (DVT).
Background: Libman Sacks Endocarditis (LSE) is a form
of sterile endocarditis. It is associated with malignancy, sys-
temic lupus erythematosus, and APLS. The etiology of LSE
appears to be an endothelial injury by cytokines in the setting
of a hypercoagulable state. There is no universal treatment
for LSE. When LSE concurs with APLS, anticoagulation is
recommended as a secondary thromboprophylaxis for APLS.
Therapy with unfractionated or low-molecular-weight hepa-
rin overlapped with warfarin remains the mainstay of the
treatment for APLS patients with venous or arterial thrombo-
sis, whereas direct oral anticoagulants are not yet rec-
ommended in such patients.
Method: A case report.
Result: A 32-year-old man with a history of hyperten-
sion, coronary artery disease, chronic kidney disease, and
bilateral lower extremity DVT on apixaban 5 mg BID pres-
ented with sudden onset dysarthria and right hemiparesis.
