Objective: To demonstrate that Bell ’s palsy may present in a
familial recurrent fashion with autoimmune disease association.
Background: Idiopathic facial palsy, also known as Bell ’s
palsy, is commonly thought as sporadic. Only a few f familial
cases have been reported and were believed to be from structural
predisposition affecting the seventh cranial nerve. We present a
case that highlights the familial inheritance pattern in one family
affecting four females without any anatomical abnormalities.
Case Presentation: We report a 35-year-old Hispanic
female who presented with right facial paralysis. She had a
total of four episodes of facial paralysis in the past at ages
6, 12, 20 and 34. The prior episodes had all resolved how-
ever the current episode had persisted for over a year. An
MRI of the facial nerve did not reveal any anatomical abnor-
mality. RF and ANA screen were elevated with further rheu-
matologic workup suggesting SLE. A detailed family history
revealed her mother, and her two sisters have similar recur-
rent facial paralysis. However neither her maternal uncles nor
her two brothers have ever been af ﬂicted. Detailed workup
for her affected family members were unrevealing.
Conclusions: Our case demonstrates that Bell ’s Palsy may
present with familial inheritance without any structural
abnormalities and supports previously proposed autosomal
dominant inheritance with variable penetrance. Furthermore,
there may be an association with Bell ’s palsy as an autoim-
mune disease with genetic factors.
