A growing number of pharmaceuticals have been identi ﬁed as
potential adjuvants to language therapy for post-stroke apha-
sia, particularly relying on cholinergic, GABAergic, and sero-
tonergic mechanisms (see Stockbridge, Handb. Clin. Neurol.:
Aphasia, 2022 for review). Given such ﬁndings, it is at least
as important to consider pharmaceuticals that may result in a
less robust recovery. Barbiturates, alpha-blockers, benzodiaze-
pines, dopamine antagonists, and norepinephrine-dopamine
reuptake inhibitors all have demonstrated deleterious effects
on post-stroke recovery generally. Moreover, recent work has
demonstrated long-term cholinergic antagonist use may con-
tribute to cognitive decline (Marzoughi et al., J. Neurochem .,
2021). Here we examined whether language recovery follow-
ing left hemisphere stroke was impeded by three candidate
pharmacological mechanisms: cholinergic (e.g., competitive
nicotinic acetylcholine (ACh) receptor antagonists, “revers-
ible” carbamate inhibitors, muscarinic receptor antagonists),
GABAergic (e.g., benzodiazepines, barbiturates), and dopami-
nergic (e.g., DAT ligands, 5-HT
2A and D 2 antagonists,
triptans).Eighty participants with left hemisphere stroke were
examined to see whether the use of one of these three classes
of medication prior to admission for acute stroke, during
their inpatient stay, or at discharge was associated with
decreased standardized differences between the acute and
chronic phases of recovery on three common measures of lan-
guage: the Western Aphasia Battery (an omnibus measure of
aphasia), Boston Naming Test (picture naming of nouns),
and the content provided when describing a picture.While
prescription of any of the candidate drugs was relatively
uncommon, drug/no drug groups were very well matched for
many common factors that impact performance. When age,
education, acute aphasia severity, and acute lesion volume
were controlled, there were no signi ﬁcant differences in stan-
dardized differences in any language measures due to cholin-
ergic, GABAergic, or dopaminergic medications in a
multivariable analysis of variance. Those who experienced a
“good recovery ” of language ( ≥10% improvement) had simi-
lar exposure to these drugs to those who experienced a poor
recovery.This work represents a ﬁrst look at the effects of
these drug classes on the recovery of language after stroke
and should not be interpreted as resolving all potential for
concern. However, it does offer modest reassurance that they
do not appear to harm recovery of post-stroke language when
given for short periods in this population. Additional pro-
spective analyses with greater speci ﬁcity are needed to fully
appreciate the relationship these drugs may have with
plasticity-dependent recovery of language in the post-stroke
period.
