Severity and Re ﬂects Underlying Ischemia in Cerebral
Small Vessel Disease
Peter Kang, MD, MSCI , Matthew R. Brier, MD, PhD,
Chunwei Ying, BS, Andria Ford, MD, MSCI, Hongyu An,
DSc, Jin-Moo Lee, MD, PhD. Washington University School of
Medicine, St. Louis, MO, USA.
Cerebral small vessel disease (CSVD), a leading cause of
stroke and cognitive impairment, manifests on neuroimaging
with discrete white matter hyperintensities (WMH) and
disrupted microstructure in normal appearing white matter
(NAWM). These imaging ﬁndings are not uniform across
patients or even within individual lesions and may belie the
heterogeneity of severity and pathologic ﬁndings in CSVD.
We hypothesized that signal intensity parameters would sig-
niﬁcantly differ in CSVD and that tissue signal heterogeneity
would be associated with signatures of hypoxia-ischemia, one
putative mechanism underlying CSVD. In this case control
cross-sectional imaging study, healthy controls (HC; n=35)
and participants with CSVD (n=27) underwent MRI using
T1-weighted MP-RAGE and T2-weighted FLAIR for
structural signal intensity quanti ﬁcation as well as
pseudocontinuous arterial spin labeling and asymmetric spin
echo to measure cerebral blood ﬂow (CBF) and oxygen
extraction fraction (OEF), respectively. Structural images
underwent intensity normalization to facilitate quanti ﬁcation
of white matter (WM) T1 and FLAIR signal intensity distri-
bution. Tissue signal properties were quanti ﬁed as the mean
and variability (i.e., heterogeneity) in signal intensity within a
tissue. We compared image intensity properties and physio-
logical measures (CBF, OEF) between groups (CSVD
vs. HC). Within the CSVD group, we examined associations
of these structural and physiological measures with lesion
burden and examined their relationships within individual
white matter lesions. Patients with CSVD had increased
