Discussion: Our study suggests that distal predominant
UE weakness is more prevalent among chronic subcortical
compared to non-subcortical stroke. However, this only rep-
resents a smaller proportion of the subcortical stroke popula-
tion and other weakness patterns may need to be identi ﬁed.
This study is limited by its retrospective nature, which may
introduce sampling biases and inter-rater variance. Thus, a
prospective study will more accurately determine prevalence
of distal predominant weakness pattern along with other
weakness patterns yet to be identi ﬁed. Findings from such
investigations could ultimately allow for a more targeted
approach to creating rehabilitation plans in the clinic, aid
physicians in stroke lesion localization, and guide where the
public health emphasis should be placed in stroke rehabilita-
