potent than LEV and is thought to have less psychiatric and
behavioral unwanted effects. However, long term
pharmacovigilance of adverse events is scarce. We aimed to
compare adverse event pro ﬁles of BRV and LEV using the
FDA Adverse Event System (FAERS).
Methods: FAERS data for adverse event pro ﬁles of BRV
and LEV were collected from January 1, 2016, to December
31, 2020, using the OpenVigil 2.1 tool and categorized into
pharmacovigilance topics using Standardized MedDRA
Queries (SMQs). Statistical analyses were conducted using
95% con ﬁdence intervals including relative reporting ratio
(RRR), proportional reporting ratio (PRR), and reporting
odds ratio (ROR) by utilizing reports of all other drugs in
FAERS as a control, providing large comparison data. Likely
true adverse events (LTAE) using the SMQ categories were
assessed with the criteria of greater than three reports, chi-
squared test with Yates ’ correction > 4, and PRR > 2. Forest
plots comparing the RORs of each psychiatric LTAE were
generated for visualization of data.
Results: Thirty LTAEs were found for LEV and twenty-two
for BRV. Total number of psychiatric LTAEs was 3919 (12%
of total) for LEV and 854 (55% of total) for BRV. LTAEs for
LEV were (ROR, 95% CI) hostility/aggression (10.787,
10.151-11.462), psychosis and psychotic disorders (4.191,
3.921-4.480), and depression without suicidality (2.049,
1.918-2.188). Psychiatric likely TAEs for BRV were psychosis
and psychotic disorders (6.921, 5.845-8.195), hostility/
aggression (6.585, 5.637-7.693), suicide/self-injury (3.198,
2.516-4.064), depression with suicidality (2.922, 2.554-3.343),
and depression without suicidality (2.738, 2.361-3.174).
Conclusions: Pharmacovigilance systems are well-
established models for long-term adverse event surveillance.
Contrary to previous studies, more psychiatric adverse events
were reported for BRV. Those with stronger associations
included psychosis and psychotic disorders, suicide/self-
injury, depression with suicidality, and depression without
suicidality. Differences were present in overall adverse event
proﬁles between LEV and BRV, warranting further research
and individualized decision making when selecting between
these two drugs.
