S161. Tri-Glial Dysregulation in Focal Cortical Dysplasia
Type II
Yu Wang, MD, PHD , Tao Yang, PHD. University of
Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA.
Somatic mutations in the mTOR signaling pathway are the
underlying genetic mechanisms of focal cortical dysplasia type
II, the most common yet poorly understood drug-resistant
epilepsy in children. Here, we demonstrate that Depdc5
somatic mutation in cortical excitatory neurons results in per-
sistent activation of microglia and astroctyes through the
C3-C3aR pathway, leading to depletion of parvalbumin
interneurons and oligodendroctyes, and impariemnt cortical
vaculatures. Microglia depletion normalizes the underlying
microenviormental pertubation and inhibits seizures in the
malformed cortex. These ﬁndings indicate that mTOR hyp-
eractivation in FCD II is associated with persistent glial dys-
regulation and identify neuroin ﬂammation as a therapeutic
