K-S107. Patient-Derived Neuron Models to Investigate
SCN2A-Related Epilepsy
Scott K. Adney, MD, PhD 1, Alfred George, Jr., MD 2.
1Northwestern University, Chicago, IL, USA, 2Northwestern
Unversity, Chicago, IL, USA.
SCN2A gain-of-function variants are implicated in early-
onset epilepsy, often in the neonatal or early infantile period.
The severity can vary from a developmental epileptic encepha-
lopathy with intractable seizures to a relatively benign self-
limited course that is not accompanied by developmental
delay. Here we probe the underlying changes to intrinsic excit-
ability in a patient with SCN2A-related epilepsy using patient-
derived excitatory neurons. Induced pluripotent stem cells
(iPSCs) were generated using blood collected from the patient
with p.M1879T variant and validated for karyotype, markers
of pluripotency, and ability to differentiate into neurons. Using
CRISPR-Cas9 editing, an isogenic control line was generated
that corrected the mutation to wildtype. iPSCs were differenti-
ated into neurons using lentiviral-expressed NGN2 and dual
SMAD/Wnt inhibition. Using whole-cell electrophysiology,
properties of intrinsic excitability were compared between the
variant line and isogenic control. Pharmacological blockers spe-
ciﬁc to neuronal sodium channels were perfused to assess dif-
ferential neuronal sodium channel activity in the two lines.
These patient-derived neurons provide a powerful tool for
understanding the epileptogenic changes produced by a novel
gain-of-function SCN2A variant.
LB-S102. Factors Associated with Refusal of Epilepsy
Surgery in Refractory Epilepsy Patients
Maryam Syed, MD , Deepti Zutshi, MD. Wayne State
University School of Medicine, Detroit, MI, USA.
Background: Epilepsy surgery is proven to be highly bene ﬁ-
cial for refractory epilepsy patients. However, patients ’ hesi-
tancy toward alternative surgical treatments for epilepsy
results in delays between initial referrals and surgical treat-
ment. Objective: To understand the demographic factors
associated with different opinions towards epilepsy surgery.
Methods: We surveyed adult epilepsy patients between
June 2020 to October 2021 at the Detroit Medical Center
using the NeuroMeasures ™ application which collected
demographic information, seizure frequency/type, and vali-
dated scales for depression, anxiety, and quality of life
(NDDI-E, GAD-7 and QOLIE-10-P). Descriptive analysis
was performed to describe patients who met criteria for
refractory epilepsy (failure of seizure control with ≥ 2 anti-
seizure medications) who did not pursue surgical options.
Results: Of 525 surveys completed, 179 met criteria for
refractory epilepsy. Twenty-seven (15.1%) patients had
already had epilepsy surgery or a neuromodulation device
implant. Sixty-eight reported discussing epilepsy surgery with
their doctors but did not wish to proceed.
Results: Of these patients, 47.1% were male and most
patients were between the ages of 18-44 years (63.2%)
followed by 45-64 years (27.9%). By race, Blacks made up
the 63.2% of the patients followed by 20.6% White and
8.8% Latinx. Focal onset with spread to bilateral tonic-clonic
(45.6%) and focal without spread (29.4%) were the most
commonly self-reported seizure type. “I am scared about hav-
ing brain surgery ” was the most selected opinion about epi-
lepsy surgery (42.6%), “I don ’t trust surgery to cure my
seizures” (14.7%) and “my friends and family are against me
having surgery ” (10.3%). Seventeen (25%) selected “Ia m
planning epilepsy surgery with my doctor ” and were mostly
to be younger patients (ages 18-44) (76.5%). Black patients
were most likely to choose “I don ’t trust surgery to cure my
epilepsy” (100%) and “I am scared about having brain sur-
gery” (69%) as their main reason for surgery refusal. Older
patients (ages > 64 years) chose “I am scared about having
epilepsy surgery ” as the main reason for surgery refusal.
Severe anxiety on the GAD-7 scale was associated with “My
friends and family are against me having surgery ”. Impaired
QoL (QOLIE-10-P score of > 25) and depression (NDDI-E
score of > 15) were reported in 58.8% and 25% of patients
overall, respectively.
Conclusion: Multiple different concerns are associated
with patients with refractory epilepsy who do not pursue sur-
gical treatment. Understanding these concerns for each
demographic group can help physicians with better counsel-
ing and educational interventions.
LB-S103. The Ictal Relationship Between Structural and
Functional Entropy in Temporal Lobe Epilepsy
Peter N. Hadar, MD, MS
1, Lohith G. Kini, MD, PhD 2,
Alfredo Lucas, SB 1, Erin Conrad, MD, MA 1, James J. Gugger,
MD, PharmD 1, Kathryn A. Davis, MD, MS 1. 1Hospital of the
University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA, USA, 2University
of California San Francisco, San Francisco, CA, USA.
Patients with drug-resistant temporal lobe epilepsy (TLE) can
achieve seizure freedom with surgery; however, the chances
of seizure freedom decrease considerably in the absence of a
clinically detectable brain lesion. Neurite Orientation Disper-
sion and Density Imaging (NODDI) is a diffusion MRI
(dMRI) technique that shows promise as a modality for iden-
tifying microstructural aberrations that cannot be visualized
on clinically acquired brain imaging. In this analysis, we
investigated the relationship between microstructural disorga-
nization inferred from NODDI and intracranial EEG
(IEEG)-based functional connectivity during seizure evolu-
tion using change in entropy. We hypothesize that higher
levels of entropy (i.e., more microstructural disorganization)
negatively correlate with iEEG synchrony during seizure evo-
lution. Twenty-three TLE patients underwent IEEG with
≥ 3 seizures, for a total of 207 seizures catalogued across all
patients. These patients and 16 controls underwent a multi-
shell dMRI acquisition. Broadband IEEG power from all
electrodes was used to calculate entropy from correlation
matrices across 5-second epochs, size-normalized, and aver-
aged across seizure and patient. Δ IEEG Entropy is the differ-
ence between ictal and pre-ictal entropy (corresponding
duration immediately before) normalized by inter-ictal
entropy (6 hours from last seizure), where a positive value
indicates an increase in entropy from baseline. Patient
NODDI maps for each metric (FICVF-neurite density,
FISO-free water, Kappa, ODI-orientation dispersion index)
