S181. Exploring Stroke Risk Factors and Outcomes in
Sexual and Gender Minority People
Michael Diaz, MD, Nicole Rosendale, MD . University of
California, San Francisco, San Francisco, CA, USA.
Background/Objectives:Cerebrovascular disease in sexual and
gender minority (SGM) people remains poorly understood.
Our primary objective was to describe the epidemiology and
outcomes in a sample of SGM people with stroke admitted to
an urban stroke center from 2016 to 2021. As a secondary
objective, we compared this group to non-SGM people with
stroke to assess for differences in risk factors or outcomes.
Methods: This was a cross-sectional study of SGM people
who were admitted to the San Francisco General Hospital
from 2016-2021 with primary diagnosis of stroke (ischemic
or hemorrhagic). Through chart review, we evaluated stroke
epidemiology and outcomes, summarizing with descriptive
statistics. We then matched one SGM person to three non-
SGM people by year of birth and year of diagnosis to com-
pare demographics, risk factors, inpatient stroke metrics, and
outcomes.
Results: A total of 26 SGM people were included in the
analysis: 20 (77%) had ischemic strokes, 5 (19%) intracere-
bral hemorrhages, and 1 (4%) subarachnoid hemorrhage.
Intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) was adminis-
tered to 1 person, and 3 people underwent thrombectomy.
Compared to non-SGM individuals (n = 78), suspected
ischemic stroke mechanisms showed a different distribution
(x
2 = 17.56, p = 0.014), with SGM people more likely to
have vasculitis, embolic stroke of undetermined source
(ESUS), and unknown etiologies. Traditional stroke risk fac-
tors were similar between the two groups, but the SGM
group appeared to have higher rates of non-traditional stroke
factors, including HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C virus. The
SGM group was more likely to be tested for these risk fac-
tors, however, which may explain these ﬁndings rather than
true differences in prevalence (X
2 = 15.80, p < 0.0001; X 2
= 11.65, p < 0.001; X 2 = 7.83, p = 0.005, respectively).
SGM people were more likely to delay presentation to the
Emergency Department ( t = 2.69, p = .009) and were more
likely to have recurrent strokes (x 2 = 4.39, p = .036) despite
similar follow up rates.
Discussion: We found that SGM people were more likely
to have recurrent strokes and delay their presentation to the
hospital as compared to non-SGM people. Policies that
emphasize collection of sexual orientation and gender identity
would enable larger studies to further understand disparities
in this population. Future research should focus on targeting
delays in presentation to the hospital and targeted stroke
reduction to prevent recurrent strokes.
