convulsive status epilepticus. EEGs can guide patient-speci ﬁc
workup, treatment, and prognostication, however delays in
obtaining EEGs can lead to missed seizure diagnoses and
suboptimal treatment. This can have deleterious effects on
patient outcomes and health-system ﬁnances.
Methods: A multidisciplinary protocol was developed for
deploying an FDA-cleared Point of Care EEG platform in
our hospital ’s emergency department and inpatient units.
The protocol educated and trained staff to obtain Point of
Care EEGs and streamline seizure workups. Analysis com-
pared 6-months of Conventional EEG against 6-months of
Point of Care EEGs.
Results: There was a 230% increase in the number of
EEGs performed at our hospital after implementing the Point
of Care protocol (post-QI, n=115 EEGs; pre-QI, n=35 EEGs).
Point of Care EEG was also associated with more con ﬁrmed
seizure diagnoses compared to Conventional EEG (18/76
vs. 0/29 patients). There was a 7.6-fold reduction in the
median time from EEG order to EEG start after QI imple-
mentation [median, (interquartile range)]: post-QI: [74 minutes
(33.5-187)] vs. pre-QI: [562 minutes (321-1034)]. Availability
of Point of Care EEG was associated with a shorter length-of-
stay [median, (interquartile range)]: post-QI: [4 days (2-9)]
vs. [pre-QI 7 days (3.5-12.5)]. In the subgroup of patients
where Point of Care EEG diagnosed seizures, additional DRG
reimbursements were captured (n=9).
Conclusion: A Point of Care EEG program led to signi ﬁ-
cant improvements in patient care through expanded access
to EEGs at our hospital. Point of Care EEG was associated
with shorter lengths-of-stay and higher rates of seizure
