M172. Asymmetry of Frontal Sleep Spindle Power in
Parkinson’s Disease with and without REM Sleep
Behavior Disorder
Vesna M. van Midden, MD 1, Lana Chahine, MD, MS 2,
Roneil G. Malkani, MD, MS 3. 1University Medical Centre
Ljubljana, Ljubljana, Slovenia, 2University of Pittsburgh,
Pittsburg, PA, USA, 3Jesse Brown Veterans Affairs Medical
Center, Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine,
Chicago, IL, USA.
Background: Sleep spindles correlate with cognitive decline
in Parkinson ’s disease (PD). Patients with isolated REM sleep
behavioural disorder (RBD) or with PD and RBD (PD
+RBD) have lower spindle density compared to PD without
RBD (PD-DEB-RWA). PD is known for its asymmetry in
neurodegeneration, yet the relationships between spindle
power asymmetry (SPA) or slow oscillations power asymme-
try (SOA) and the presence of RBD in PD have not been
assessed. We compared SPA and SOA in PD-DEB-RWA
patients to PD patients with clinical (dream enactment
behaviour (PD+DEB)) or electrophysiological ﬁndings of
RBD (REM without atonia (PD+RWA)).
Methods: In this cross-sectional study, we conducted
power spectral analysis on polysomnographic electroencepha-
logram data. The sample consisted of 55 individuals with
PD: 10 PD-DEB-RWA and 45 PD+RBD (8 without DEB
but with RWA (PD+RWA); 13 with DEB but without
RWA (PD+DEB); and 24 with both DEB and RWA (PD
+DEB+RWA)). Relative spindle power (rSP; 11-15Hz) and
relative slow oscillations power (rSO; 0,5-1,5Hz) were calcu-
lated as SP and SO power, respectively, divided by total
power (0,5-30Hz) and averaged across sleep stages N2-N3.
To limit variability, we only used the ﬁrst sleep cycle. Frontal
and central SPA and SOA were calculated as the absolute
value of the differences between mean relative values for left
and right; higher values translate to greater asymmetry.
Mann-Whitney test was used to compare SPA and SOA
