Objective: Here we provide a summary of demographic
and genetic information for enrolled participants in the
GEnetic counseling Through VIRTUAL visits in
Parkinson ’s disease (GET VIRTUAL-PD) clinical trial.
The GET VIRTUAL-PD study aims to assess the safety
and ef ﬁcacy of a web-based pre-test genetic counseling tool
called the Interactive, Multimedia Approach to Genetic
counseling to INform and Educate in Parkinson ’sD i s e a s e
(IMAGINE-PD).
Background: Mutations in GBA and LRRK2 are detected
in approximately 10% of patients with Parkinson ’s disease
(PD), and may in ﬂuence eligibility for patients accessing
forthcoming GBA and LRRK2-targeted therapies. As
patients’ interest in and access to genetic testing expands,
alternative genetic counseling models and novel tools are
needed.
Methods: Every patient with PD at the UPenn
Parkinson’s Disease and Movement Disorders Center (>2000
patients) is offered enrollment into the Molecular Integration
in Neurologic Diagnosis (MIND) biobanking initiative,
which includes research-based genetic screening for 22 vari-
ants in GBA and LRRK2. Eligible participants are identi ﬁed,
then offered enrollment into the GET VIRTUAL-PD study
for con ﬁrmation testing and disclosure of genetic results. Par-
ticipants are randomized by factorial design into one of two
groups for pre-test information (pre-test genetic counseling
via videoconference visit with the genetic counselor (GC) vs.
pre-test self-guided review of the web-based IMAGINE-PD
tool) and one of two groups for result disclosure (videocon-
ference vs. phone call with the GC). The primary outcome
measures are patient knowledge, satisfaction, and test-related
distress via survey responses at 3 and 6 months post-
disclosure.
Results: Between January 2021 and April 8, 2022,
394 MIND participants were identi ﬁed as eligible and con-
tacted about the follow-up disclosure study, GET
VIRTUAL-PD. Of these, 162 were successfully enrolled.
To date, 17 have withdrawn or deferred study visits, while
104 have successfully completed their genetic test result dis-
closure visit. Of those having completed a disclosure visit,
16% carried a clinically-con ﬁrmed variant in GBA or
LRRK2. Enrollment is ongoing for a target of
320 participants.
Conclusions: In anticipation of future GBA and
LRRK2-targeted therapies, patients and providers are showing
increased interest in genetic testing. If demonstrated to be safe
and effective, tools such as the IMAGINE-PD online resource
may allow for safe and effective communication of genetic
information to affected individuals at clinic-wide scale.
