levels, and NK cell surface marker expression was assessed
using ﬂow cytometry, and changes in immune levels were
assessed in control and ALS subjects over time. Next, in ALS
subjects a linear mixed effect model - adjusted for covariates
such as age, sex, site of onset, and El Escorial criteria - was
used to correlate changes in ALSFRS-R with changes occur-
ring up to six months before or afterward in the immune sys-
tem. Consistent with our previous reports, immune cells such
as CD4 T cells decreased over time, as did several markers
expressed by NK cells such as CD11a, CD11b, CD38,
CX3CR1, and NKp30. Interestingly, we found that increases
in NK cell markers CD11a, CD38, and CX3CR1 were asso-
ciated with smaller subsequent decreases in ALSFRS-R - indi-
cating slower disease progression - while increases in NKp30
were associated with larger subsequent decreases in
ALSFRS-R. Together these ﬁndings demonstrate that not
only do changes in speci ﬁc immune metrics predicts subse-
quent ALS progression but that NK cells may play a more
