Background: SCAs are a group of heredofamilial neurode-
generative diseases that predominantly involve the cerebellum
and spinocerebellar tracts, but neuropathy, sensory
ganglionopathy, degeneration of corticospinal tracts and
lower motor neurons may be present in certain subtypes.
The most common SCA types are SCA3 followed by SCA2
and SCA6. While SCA6 is characterized by a pure, late onset
ataxia, SCA2 and SCA3 are also characterized by involvement
of corticospinal tracts, dorsal root ganglia, and motor neu-
rons. As involvement of the noncerebellar systems play a role
in the morbidity of these patients, biomarkers which assess
involvement of motor and sensory systems are needed to test
