marker of disease activity in SCA subtypes which have
involvement of corticospinal tract and sensory pathways
(i.e. SCA2 and SCA3) in addition to cerebellar degeneration.
Subjects and Methods: In this proof of concept, single
center, cross sectional study, we enrolled the following
patients: SCA2 (N=14), SCA3 (N=15), SCA6 (N=27), and
neurotypical controls (N=29). Mean, range of age, and
female percentile were: SCA2: 41.5, 22-69, 36%; SCA3:
53.7, 30-75, 67%; SCA6: 59.5, 44-86, 56%; and normal
controls: 47.5, 22-79, 45%. Compared to the neurotypical
group, the SCA6 patient group was older (p=0.015); there
were no other signi ﬁcant differences between the SCA groups
and the neurotypical group. IMC- βγ was calculated using
surface electromyography signals obtained simultaneously
from biceps and brachioradialis during the task of ﬂexion and
pronation of the forearm, antigravity.
Results: The mean IMC- βγ amplitudes +/- SD were
SCA2: 0.039 +/- 0.047, SCA3: 0.020 +/- 0.029, SCA6:
0.064 +/- 0.094, and neurotypical control subjects: 0.043 +/-
0.036; IMC- βγ amplitude was signi ﬁcantly lower in SCA3
patients compared to neurotypical control subjects (P<0.05),
with an area under the ROC curve of 0.76, implying a fair
ability to distinguish between neurotypical and SCA-3 sub-
jects based exclusively on IMC- βγ measurements.
