Objective: To describe baseline characteristics of patients
with Parkinson ’s disease (PD) enrolled in the Opicapone
Treatment Initiation Open-Label Study (OPTI-ON).
Background: Opicapone is an oral, selective, once-daily
catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor used as
adjunctive treatment to carbidopa/levodopa (CD/LD) in
patients with PD experiencing “OFF”-episodes. United States
(US) FDA approval was granted in April 2020 based on results
from two global, double-blind, Phase III studies: BIPARK-1
and BIPARK-2. The aim of the current OPTI-ON study is to
evaluate “real world” patient characteristics, treatment patterns,
and safety/tolerability of opicapone in the US.
