Design/Methods: OPTI-ON is a prospective, open-label,
single-arm, multicenter, observational, longitudinal
(6-month) study of patients with PD experiencing “OFF”-
episodes who were prescribed opicapone as adjunctive treat-
ment to CD/LD. Patients were enrolled between March
2021 and February 2022. Clinicians managed patients per
standard of care and had a choice to initiate opicapone treat-
ment and administer assessments either during in-person
ofﬁce visits or virtually (telemedicine). Clinician-reported
outcomes (e.g., PD Status Questionnaire, PD medications)
and patient-reported outcomes were obtained at baseline and
throughout patient follow-up for 6 months.
Results: In total, 243 patients with PD and “OFF”-
episodes were enrolled from 30 sites in the US. Mean ( /C6SD)
age was 67.9 ( /C69.0) years, 36.2% were female, 89.7% not
Hispanic or Latino, 92.2% white, and mean PD duration
was 8.12 years. At baseline (treatment initiation), 1.7% of
patients had Mild PD, 24.7% Mild to Moderate PD, 50.6%
Moderate PD, 11.9% Moderate to Advanced PD, and 2.1%
Advanced PD as measured by the PD Status Questionnaire.
Most (88.9%) baseline visits were in-person. All patients
were initiated on once-daily opicapone 50mg. At baseline,
patients utilized a variety of CD/LD regimens, including
immediate-release CD/LD tablets (Sinemet) (49.4% of
patients) and extended-release CD/LD capsules (Rytary)
(22.2% of patients). 11.1% of patients used entacapone
before enrollment and were switched to opicapone. 2.1% of
patients had Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS) at baseline.
Conclusions: Opicapone was added to a variety of CD/LD
formulations, including over 20% of patients utilizing Rytary.
While the study was conducted during the ongoing pandemic
and investigators could initiate treatment via telemedicine, most
investigators elected to initiate patients on opicapone with an
in-person visit. Findings from OPTI-ON will further character-
ize the PD population treated with opicapone and real-world
clinician- and patient-reported outcomes data in the US will
complement results from previous controlled clinical trials.
