types of PD.
Design/Methods: Charts of 176 veterans with Parkinson ’s
disease at the Jesse Brown VA Hospital in Chicago, Illinois
were reviewed in detail. We recorded presence or absence of
35 different motor and non-motor symptoms, age of symp-
tom onset and diagnosis, diagnosis of PTSD, and H&Y
scale.
Results: PTSD diagnosis along with PD, compared to PD
without PTSD, was associated with: more neuropsychiatric
symptoms (3.8 vs 2.3, p-value (p) <0.00001); more sleep
symptoms (1.5 symptoms vs 1.1, p= 0.03), less motor symp-
toms (3 vs 3.5, p= 0.02); increased presence of depression
(78% vs 55%, p=0.004), anxiety (79% vs 53%, p=0.002),
apathy (71% vs 46%, p=0.012), panic attacks (59% vs 29%,
p=0.01), aggression (79% vs 46%, p=0.001), pain (84% vs
