Background: Parkinson’s disease (PD), multiple system atro-
phy (MSA), and progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) are
neurodegenerative diseases with overlapping syndromes that
lack effective neuroprotective treatments. New imaging tools
are needed to quantify mechanistically important features of
these diseases and to assist clinical diagnosis and therapeutic
trial designs. PD, MSA, and PSP each exhibit neuromelanin
loss and iron deposition across a range of brain structures,
but disease-speci ﬁc spatial patterns of change have not been
clariﬁed in vivo . No study to our knowledge has simulta-
neously acquired neuromelanin-sensitive MRI and (iron-sen-
sitive) R2 * relaxometry data to investigate this, and here we
report results with this approach.
Methods: All research was IRB approved. 82 patients with
neurodegenerative parkinsonism (PD=54, PSP=11, parkinso-
nian MSA (MSA-P)=17) and 40 controls were recruited from
The Emory Clinic and scanned on a Siemens Prisma 3T
MRI system. A magnetization-transfer-prepared 2D-gradient-
echo neuromelanin-sensitive sequence was acquired and sub-
stantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) and locus coeruleus
(LC) volumes were extracted using a highly reproducible
approach (Langley et al, MAGMA, 2017). Multi-echo
gradient-echo data was acquired and R2 * values were deter-
mined in brainstem and basal ganglia structures using a
reproducible approach (Feng, Z Med Phys, 2018). Extracted
