School,, Boston, MA, USA, 3Massachusetts General Hospital
and Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA, 4Department
of Behavioral Medicine, Jose Reyes Memorial Medical Center,
Manila, Philippines.
Background: X-linked dystonia parkinsonism (XDP) is a
neurogenetic combined movement disorder involving both
parkinsonism and dystonia. For clinical trial readiness, rater-
independent, quantitative assessments of motor function are
urgently needed. Complex, overlapping phenotypes result in
difﬁculties in clinical rating scale assessment. We performed
sensor-based motion analysis in XDP to quantitatively char-
acterize disease phenomenology and as a potential clinical
trial endpoint.
Methods: 10 symptomatic XDP patients and 4 healthy
controls underwent motion sensor analysis with a standard-
ized examination. Disease severity was assessed with the Uni-
ﬁed Parkinson ’s Disease Rating Scale (MDS-UPDRS) and
Burke-Fahn-Marsden scale (BFM). We assessed speci ﬁc
MDS-UPDRS/BFM motor tasks using clinical scores as the
dependent, segmented motion signals captured with wearable
sensor data and derived a set of data features for the move-
ments’ characterization. Data feature selection and projection
algorithms were used to inspect the data feature space and
machine learning (ML) models were trained and validated to
demonstrate the ability of derived data features to estimate
clinical scores from sensor data. We performed sensor-based
gait analysis, deriving gait spatio-temporal parameters and
assessed the suitability to discriminate between different gait
severities.
Results: XDP patients were at varying stages of disease
(duration 3-28 years) and severity of dystonia (BFM 0-43.5)
and parkinsonism (MDS-UPDRS 13-48). Projections rev-
ealed distinct clusters of sensor-derived data features
corresponding to different MDS-UPDRS scores across con-
sidered tasks ( ﬁnger-to-nose, hand pronation/supination, leg
agility, foot tapping, straight walking), predicting MDS-
UPDRS scores with mean accuracy across tasks of 72.2%
(63-81%). Projections of dystonia-speci ﬁc data features
highlighted cluster separation between controls and XDP
with/without dystonia. ML-based analysis showed that the
trained model correctly classi ﬁed 78.3% (60%-94%) of sam-
ples across the considered tasks. The projections/ML-gait
analysis data revealed that gait spatio-temporal parameters
