City, UT, USA, 2ARUP Institute for Clinical and Experimental
Pathology, Salt Lake City, UT, USA.
Testing for antibodies to myelin oligodendrocyte glycopro-
tein (anti-MOG) IgG has been of much interest in the
recent past, with several groups comparing live versus ﬁxed
cell-based assays, as well as other available assays, for diag-
nostic accuracy. Here, we describe the agreement between
a ﬁx e dc e l l - b a s e di m m u n oﬂuorescence assay performed
routinely at ARUP Laboratories and a live cell-based
ﬂuorescence-activated cell sorting assay performed at Mayo
Clinical Laboratories. We evaluated inter-assay agreement
between the two tests in over 300 paired samples sent to
ARUP over a period of 2 years. We also describe the clinical
characteristics of the 17 cases which were seen within the
University of Utah Healthcare System and were assessed for
