S216. Mechanism Underlying Persistent Behavior
Deﬁcits in Mouse Models of Anti-NMDA Receptor
Encephalitis
Jing Zhou, Ph.D.
1, Ariele Green ﬁeld, MD, Ph.D. 1, Rita
Loudermilk, B.S. 1, Christopher Bartley, MD, Ph.D. 1, Baouyen
Tran, Ph.D. 1, Chao Zhao, M.S 2, Hong Wang, B.S. 1, Michael
Wilson, MD 1, Samuel Pleasure, MD, Ph.D. 1. 1University of
California, San Francisco, San Francisco, CA, USA, 2Children’s
Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA, USA.
Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis characterized by anti-
NMDA receptor autoantibodies in cerebrospinal ﬂuid, mani-
fests with prominent sensory-motor coordination de ﬁcits
amongst a variety of other neurologic symptoms. We have
demonstrated that NMDA receptor controls callosal projec-
tions in primary somatosensory cortex (S1), which is a key
structure for integrating sensory-motor information. Here,
we generated monoclonal antibodies from an anti-NMDA
receptor encephalitis patient and identi ﬁed antibodies against
GluN1 and GluN2A subunits. In mice, the anti-GluN1 anti-
body dramatically increased callosal projections, and both
antibodies permanently disrupted the morphology of callosal
axon terminals in S1. Most importantly, the disruption of
the S1 circuit during development caused sensory-motor de ﬁ-
cits in adulthood, and the severity of the de ﬁcit was positively
correlated with axon terminal alterations in S1. Our study
conﬁrmed the central role of anti-GluN1 and anti-GluN2A
autoantibodies in the pathogenesis of anti-NMDA receptor
encephalitis and reveals the mechanism underlying persistent
sensory-motor de ﬁcits in children. This is relevant to
the effects of this syndrome in pediatric and newborn
patients and may also shed light on the protracted
neurocognitive de ﬁcits experienced by anti-NMDA receptor
encephalitis patients even after resolution of the acute phase
of the illness.
S217. Mental Health Disorders in the MS Population
Samiksha Srivastava, MD , Zena Azo, Bachelor Degree, Fen
Bao, Masters Degree, Adam Lazar, Bachelor Degree, Emily Pelc,
Bachelor Degree, Amanda Reyes, MD, Carla Santiago-
Martinez, Masters Degree, Mahmoud Elkhooly, MD, Jacob
Rube, MD, Evanthia Bernitsas, MD. Wayne State University,
Detroit, MI, USA.
Objective: To examine the demographic prevalence of men-
tal health disorders, including anxiety, depression, schizo-
phrenia, and bipolar disorder in Multiple sclerosis
(MS) populations.
Background: In MS active brain in ﬂammation and exces-
sive production of proin ﬂammatory cytokines and neu-
rodegeneration may contribute to anxiety, depression, and
psychosis. Mental health disorders can negatively affect the
quality of life and result in job loss, increased divorce rate,
and family con ﬂict. Therefore, professional counseling, sup-
port groups, and treatment can be helpful in managing men-
tal health disorders.
Methodology: This is a retrospective analysis of
146 patients diagnosed with MS that aims to identify those
with mental comorbidities. Data were abstracted from elec-
tronic medical records by one reviewer and veri ﬁed by a sec-
ond reviewer. A descriptive and Chi-square analysis was
performed using SPSS v26.
Results: We identify ﬁfty patients (34.2%) who were diag-
nosed with mental health disorders. Among them, 37 were
women, 13 were men, 27 were whites, and 23 were non-
whites. Thirty (60%) patients had anxiety, 18 (36%) had
depression, 1(2%) had schizophrenia and 1 (2%) had bipolar
disorder. The average age for diagnosing anxiety was 47-years
old, 54-year-old with depression, 26-year-old with schizo-
phrenia, and 45-year-old with bipolar disorder.
Conclusions: Our ﬁndings suggest a high prevalence of
mental health comorbidities in MS patients with anxiety
being the most common followed by depression. Interest-
ingly, psychotic disorders are more frequent in MS patients
than in the general population. Periodic screening may be of
beneﬁt. Future studies with larger samples are warranted.
