encephalomyelitis (PEM), the highest prevalence being bron-
chial small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC). However, PEM
occurs in less than 1% of patients diagnosed with a malig-
nancy. The existing criteria for diagnosis of PEM relies
heavily on testing for antibodies, which include but are not
limited to anti-Hu, anti-Ta, anti-CV2, anti-Ma, anti-gamma-
Aminobutyric acid (GABA) B receptor, and anti-N-methyl-
D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor and cross-react with self-
antigens in the neurons and muscles. However, current litera-
ture also shows that absence of these antibodies does not rule
out paraneoplastic encephalitis. Herein we present a unique
case of a seronegative paraneoplastic encephalitis in a forty-
nine-year-old female with SCLC.
Case Report: The patient was brought in by a family
member due to worsening cognitive impairment and altered
mental status. Her chest x-ray was notable for a dense con-
solidation/mass in the right upper and lower lobe. The ﬁnd-
ings in addition to bulky mediastinal lymphadenopathy
were further highlighted on a chest computed tomography
(CT). An endobronchial biopsy was performed with pathol-
ogy con ﬁrming a small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma. Her
head CT revealed no intracranial pathology, brain magnetic
resonance imaging (MRI) con ﬁrmed no acute infarct, and
no intracranial mass. A lumbar puncture was performed
which did not suggest active infection or antibody mediated
autoimmune process. At that ti me systemic antibiotics and
antivirals were cessated. A twent y-four-hour video electroen-
cephalogram (VEEG) was only notable for mild to moder-
ate diffuse background slowing and negative for any
epileptiform activity. Chemot herapy was started in the hope
that seronegative PEM was the underlying etiology. After
the initiation of chemotherapy, the patient ’s mental status
gradually improved, and a full course was completed
outpatient.
Discussion: Given the remarkable improvement with sys-
temic chemotherapy the patient was given a diagnosis of sero-
negative PEM. We suggest a more nuanced clinical approach
to the diagnosis when considering paraneoplastic encephalitis
that transcends antibody testing. We recommend that once a
malignancy has been con ﬁrmed and other potential underly-
ing disease processes have been ruled out that empiric chemo-
therapy be used to treat seronegative PEM.
