Robert Fox, MD 2, Daniel Ontaneda, MD, PhD 2. 12Cleveland
Clinic, Cleveland, OH, USA.
Background: Thalamic volume is associated with physical
disability and cognitive impairment in MS and is thought to
be affected by white matter lesion burden affecting afferent/
efferent tracts. Individual thalamic subregions are associated
with distinct neurological functions due to their connectivity
and modulation of pathways.
Objectives: To examine whether baseline thalamic subre-
gional volumes can predict change in neuroperformance mea-
sures in patients with progressive MS.
Methods: Thalamic subregional volumes, using Multiple
Automatically Generated Templates (MAGeT) brain segmen-
tation, were calculated in 214 subjects with progressive MS
over 2-years with 6-month interval follow-up from the
SPRINT-MS trial. Linear mixed-effect models of all normal-
ized baseline subregional volumes with covariates (age, sex,
race, and disease duration) were used to predict change in
neuroperformance measures: Expanded Disability Status
Scale (EDSS), timed 25-foot walk (T25FW), 9-hole peg test
(9HPT; dominant and nondominant hands separately evalu-
ated), and Symbol Digit Modalites Test (SDMT). Sub-
thalamic regions that were signi ﬁcant (p < 0.05) after
correcting for false discovery rate were noted.
Results: Larger baseline volumes of the lateral dorsal and
pulvinar thalamic subregions signi ﬁcantly predicted lower
EDSS. Larger medial dorsal and smaller pulvinar volume
predicted longer T25FW.
Conclusions: The direction of the inverse relationship
between lateral dorsal and pulvinar thalamic subregional
