The mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) is a central
kinase involved in cellular growth and proliferation. While
the roles of mTOR in cancer have been extensively studied,
mTOR also has key roles in neurological disorders such as
autism spectrum disorder and epilepsy. One key regulator of
mTOR activity in the brain is the Tuberous Sclerosis Com-
plex (TSC), formed by TSC1, TSC2, and TBC1D7, which
inhibits mTOR signaling. To understand the effects of
increased mTOR activity in neurons, we performed single
cell sequencing on the cortex of an animal model of TSC,
which has a 90% reduction of TSC2 protein in a subset of
neurons. We found that layer 5 pyramidal neurons showed
substantial down-regulation of synapse-associated genes.
