Objective: Aicardi Goutières Syndrome (AGS) is a heritable
interferonopathy that results in variable neurologic disability.
Key variables (e.g. genotype and age at onset) only partially
correlate with neurologic function, which can range from iso-
lated spastic paraparesis to profound global developmental
delay. As therapies are developed, it is critical to develop and
validate tools to better measure the impact of disease. The
purpose of this study is to characterize neuromotor disability
in AGS using the Gross Motor Function Measure-88
(GMFM-88), a traditional outcome assessment validated in
non-AGS populations and the AGS scale which developed
for the AGS population.
Methods: 68 subjects with molecularly-conﬁrmed AGS were
enrolled. Participants’ sex, age, genotype, age at onset of disease
were collected. Neurologic performance on the AGS scale
(score range 0-11) and the gross motor function measure
(GMFM)-88 (range from 0 to 100%) were collected. From the
GMFM-88, a total score (maximum score 100) as well as sub-
dimension scores (lying and rolling, sitting, crawling/kneeling,
standing, and walking/running/jumping) were generated.
Results: In total, the GMFM-88 was administered to
68 subjects [n=38 males; mean age at GMFM-88 assessment:
6.3 (5.9) years, range: 0.2 - 22.2 years]. This cohort represen-
ted a range of neurologic severity, as assessed by the AGS
scale (median score= 4, interquartile range/IQR: 6). Perfor-
mance on the AGS Scale correlated with total GMFM-88
scores (Spearman Correlation R
2 0.826). The mean
(SD) performance on the GMFM-88 scores was 26.25
(31.63). Of the 68 participants, the performance on 45 assess-
ments (66%) was less than 20, which is in the bottom regis-
ter of scores. To assess the variability within each genotypic
subcohort, the interquartile ranges were compared; TREX1-
related AGS had the smallest range (IQR: 4.23) versus
SAMHD1-related AGS, which had the largest interquartile
range (IQR: 85.5).
Conclusions: Our results suggest that overall, the
GMFM-88 can be used to assess neurologic function in
AGS, and performance on this instrument is proportional to
the AGS scale. Within a subcohort of individuals with severe
motor impairments, interpretation of the GMFM-88 may be
compromised by ﬂoor effects. Among individuals with
moderate-to-high functioning AGS (AGS score of
>3), how-
ever, the GMFM-88 assessment may be a useful tool to pro-
vide for neurologic assessments. This work underscores the
need for additional validation of outcome assessments to
address the dynamic range of function found in AGS.
