Despite the importance of prenatal hypoxia as a cause of neu-
rodevelopmental disabilities, there is a mechanistic gap in
understanding why hypoxia causes lasting functional de ﬁcits
even when there is no signi ﬁcant cell death at the time of
injury. One hypothesis to account for lasting de ﬁcits is that
prenatal hypoxia alters the epigenome of different cell types
