assist in establishing diagnoses that guide treatment.
Objectives: To describe a multi-institutional collaboration
designed for in-depth investigation of undiagnosed neuro-
inﬂammatory and neuroinfectious diseases.
Methods: Participants with laboratory or radiological evi-
dence of neuroin ﬂammation and without an established diag-
nosis following routine clinical work up, were accepted to the
protocol and underwent a standardized in-depth investigation
at one of the collaborating clinical centers including clinical
examination, MRIs and blood and CSF analysis using
advanced clinical and research assays based on a newly
established algorithm for assessment of patients and
processing and storage of samples across multiple labs
involved. Cases were discussed in a monthly interdisciplinary
conference.
Results: During 2021, we recruited 28 participants with
challenging idiopathic neuroin ﬂammatory syndromes. Immu-
nophenotyping was completed by multicolor ﬂow cytometry
and single-cell RNA sequencing, with each patient showing a
unique immune pattern, allowing for improved understand-
ing of the syndrome and new choice of treatments (e.g., a
case of treatment-refractory in ﬂammatory cerebellar lesions
treated with abatacept due to high levels of CTLA-4, with a
good response). Samples were also analyzed using unbiased
and capture sequencing methods, and autoantibody and viral
antibody discovery using phage immunoprecipitation
sequencing (PhIP-Seq) and peptide microarrays. ln one case
