K-S121. Acute Flaccid Myelitis: Role for Enterovirus-
Induced Nuclear Pore Complex Dysfunction in
Pathogenesis
Matthew J. Elrick, M.D., Ph.D.
1, Malavika Jayaram, M.S. 1,
Jeffrey Rothstein, M.D., Ph.D. 2. 1Kennedy Krieger Institute,
Baltimore, MD, USA, 2Johns Hopkins School of Medicine,
Baltimore, MD, USA.
Acute ﬂaccid myelitis (AFM) is an emerging polio-like illness
that causes paralysis in children via toxicity to spinal motor
neurons. AFM is caused by enteroviruses, and Enterovirus
D68 (EV-D68) has been responsible for multiple worldwide
outbreaks of AFM in recent years. The enterovirus life cycle
involves the disruption of nucleocytoplasmic transport, all-
owing RNA binding proteins that typically reside in the
nucleus to participate in viral replication in the cytoplasm,
and disrupting the nuclear export of host mRNA. Similar
deﬁcits in nucleocytoplasmic transport have been implicated
in neurodegenerative disorders, most notably the motor neu-
ron disease Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS). We there-
fore hypothesize that enterovirus-induced nucleocytoplasmic
transport dysfunction contributes to the selective motor neu-
ron toxicity that occurs in AFM. Here, we demonstrate that
EV-D68 proteases cleave multiple nucleoporins. This process
disrupts the permeability barrier of the nuclear pore complex
for proteins and diminishes the ef ﬁciency of RNA export.
EV-D68 proteases are suf ﬁcient to cause toxicity to human
iPS-derived motor neurons. These ﬁndings begin to elucidate
the role of the nuclear pore complex in AFM pathogenesis
and suggest possible targets for neuroprotective therapies.
K-S122. Loss of Myocyte Enhancer Factor 2C (MEF2C)
Leads to Microglial Dysfunction in an Autism Model
Emily Hansen, MS, Anna S. Warden, PhD, Cristina Mora,
MS, Gabriela Ramirez, n/a, Gabriela Ramirez, n/a, Samantha
Mak, n/a, Sanjana Narayan, n/a, Samantha Trescott, BS,
Shreya Shriram, BS, Zahara Keulen, MS, Christopher K. Glass,
MD PhD, Nicole G. Coufal, MD PhD . University of
California, San Diego, San Diego, CA, USA.
Microglia act as the primary innate immune effector of the
brain and have increasingly been found to in ﬂuence neuro-
development and brain maturation. The microglial cell fate is
heavily reliant on other brain cell types and is regulated by a
network of environmentally dependent transcription factors,
one of which is myocyte enhancer factor 2C (MEF2C).
The role of MEF2C in neurodevelopment is of particular
interest as loss of one allele of MEF2C results in a monoge-
netic autism spectrum disorder known as MEF2C
haploinsuf ﬁciency syndrome (MHS). Patients with MHS
exhibit a severe autism spectru m disorder characterized by
intellectual disability, epilepsy, social and speech de ﬁcits.
To investigate the role of MEF2C in microglial develop-
ment, we generated CRISPR modi ﬁed induced pluripotent
stem cells (iPSCs) and iPSC derived microglia carrying
biallelic knockouts of MEF2C, knockout of MEF2C in
one allele (haploinsuf ﬁcient), and isogenic controls. We
postulate that MEF2C haploinsuf ﬁciency causes an altered
microglia phenotype that contributes to impaired brain
development in MHS patients . Loss of microglial MEF2C
results in an amoeboid morphology coupled with elevated
CD68, altered lysosomal activity, and elevated basal release
of proin ﬂammatory cytokines, all characteristic of micro-
glial activation. Ingenuity pathway analysis (IPA) and
weighted gene co-expression network analysis (WGCNA)
of transcriptomic data identi ﬁed in ﬂammatory pathways as
the primary difference between MEF2C knockouts and
controls. The ameboid morphology observed in in vitro
MEF2C knockout microglia is recapitulated in cerebral
organoid coculture and a murine xenotransplantation
model. Altogether we identify an essential role for MEF2C
in microglial function and a potential role in the pathogen-
esis of MHS, a monogenetic autism syndrome.
