2UC Davis, Davis, CA, USA, 3NINDS, Bethesda, MD, USA,
4FCM - UNICAMP, Campinas, Brazil.
The prenatal brain is uniquely susceptible to viral infections.
Such infections threaten one in four pregnancies and can cause
severe neurodevelopmental disorders if not fetal demise. It is
noteworthy that different viruses lead to strikingly similar clini-
cal outcomes, possibly due to overlapping viral neuropathology
and the host ’s common neuronal response. Such a reality calls
for a much-deeper understanding of the corresponding neuro-
biology, which provides the context for this research, with a
long view toward therapeutic intervention.
A key regulator of the host ’s common neuroviral response is
the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR) through essential
roles in viral autophagy that affect viral clearance and neuronal
survival. There is unsettled controversy, however, about
whether mTOR expression and viral autophagy are key defenses
against, or drivers of, viral replication and neuronal injury. In
an attempt to bring greater clarity to this issue, our study rigor-
ously tests the guiding hypothesis that neurodevelopmental
gene expression affects prenatal viral susceptibility toward brain
injury. Using cellular, organoid, and human systems, we have
new preliminary data connecting mTORwith a novel neuroviral
response gene, Hexosaminidase B (HEXB), and suggesting new
cellular functions for this genetic complex in activating prenatal
viral autophagy to prevent brain injury.
Among our results, bioinformatic analyses of clinical out-
comes identiﬁed 3,098 candidate susceptibility genes, cluster-
ing into 200 neurogenetic pathways. Candidate genes were
then screened by performing whole exome sequencing on
22 case and 17 control participants. From among 1,400+
