LB-S115. Neurologic and Neurocognitive Sequelae
Following COVID-19 in Lima, Peru
Hanalise V. Huff, MD, MPH
1, Carla Villanueva-Colina,
MD2, Monica M. Diaz, MD 3, Davidson H. Hamer, MD 4,
Igor J. Koralnik, MD 5, Patricia J. Garcia, MD, PhD, MPH 2.
1Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Boston, MA,
USA, 2Universidad Peruana Cayetano Heredia, Lima, Peru,
3University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill, Chapel Hill, NC,
USA, 4Boston University School of Public Health, Department
of Global Health, Boston, MA, USA, 5Northwestern University
Feinberg School of Medicine, Department of Neurology,
Chicago, IL, USA.
Background: SARS-CoV-2 has been found to cause neuro-
logic manifestations in addition to typical respiratory symp-
toms. Peru saw about 3.6 million cases and 213 thousand
deaths with Lima having the highest percentage of both.
Patients hospitalized with mild to moderate disease were
found to present with acute neurologic symptoms, however,
there is limited research on clinical persistence of neurologic
manifestations of post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infec-
tion (Neuro-PASC) in Peru.
Methods: Data were collected during a prospective obser-
vational cohort study consisting of follow-up of survivors of
COVID-19 at more than 90 days following initial infection.
Visits included neurologic history and symptom question-
naires, screening questionnaire for depression (Patient
Health Questionnaire-9 or PHQ-9) and anxiety (General
Anxiety Disease-7 or GAD-7) both using a cutoff score of
10, a tablet-based neurocognitive assessment (NCA) (NIH
Toolbox Neurocognitive assessment) and paper-based NCA
(Rowland Universal Dementia Assessment Screening or
RUDAS) with scores 25 or lower indicating cognitive
deﬁcit.
Results: 41 patients completed their ﬁrst visit 90-270 days
following disease onset. 2/41 patients were hospitalized. 51%
met criteria for depression and 44% met criteria for GAD.
On RUDAS, only 9% (4/44) met criteria for cognitive
deﬁcit. On the tablet-based NCA, the following are percent-
ages of those with t scores below average for each cognitive
domain based on age, sex and education-matched norms:
(22/41) 53.7% for processing speed, 56% (23/41) for atten-
tion, 18/41 (44%) for executive function, and 22/41
(53.7%) for working memory. 80% (33/41) reported subjec-
tively feeling less than 100% and 44% (18/41) felt 75% or
less compared to pre-COVID-19 baseline.
Discussion: Here we demonstrate the presence of
neurocognitive disfunction compounded by important men-
tal health impact in a Peruvian population months after
COVID-19. About half met criteria for depression and anxi-
ety. 44-56% scored below average on the four cognitive
domains tested, with worse performance on attention. Inter-
estingly, 80% of patients reported not feeling back to their
pre-COVID-19 baseline. Major limitations of this study
include the lack of control group and the variable length of
follow up between disease and ﬁrst visit.
Conclusion: T h e r ei sal a r g eb u r d e no fn e u r o l o g i c ,
neurocognitive and psychological disease in survivors of
COVID-19 in Lima, Peru and it is important to study outcomes
longitudinally to predict and better accommodate long term
physical, social and economic effects of SARS-CoV-2 infection.
