Background: Analyses of kinetic defects of adult εAChR
mutations causing congenital myasthenic syndrome (CMS)
have guided therapy based on individual pathogenic mecha-
nism. However, up to date, the af ﬁnity of ACh binding and
gating ef ﬁciency of human fetal γAChR have not been char-
acterized, and no kinetic study of fetal γAChR mutations
causing Escobar syndrome has been reported.
Methods: Whole exome and Sanger sequencing, expres-
sion of AChR in HEK cells, α-bungarotoxin binding assay,
single channel patch-clamp recordings, and maximum likeli-
hood analysis of channel kinetics.
Results: We identi ﬁed compound heterozygous mutations
(listed with legacy nomenclature) in the AChR γ and ε sub-
units in 3 Escobar syndrome patients (1, 2, and 3) and
3 CMS patients (4, 5, and 6), respectively. Each Escobar
patient carries γP121R variant with the second variant of
γV221Afs*44 in patients 1 and 2, and γY63* in patient
3. Three CMS patients share εP121T variant at equivalent res-
idue of γP121 with second variant εR20W, εG-8R, and
εY15H in patient 4, 5, and 6, respectively. Surface expression
of γP121R- and εP121T-AChR were 80% and 138% of the
corresponding wild-type AChR, whereas εR20W, εG-8R, and
εY15H reduced receptor expression to 27%, 35%, and 30%
of wild-type εAChR, respectively, and γV221Afs*44 and
γY63* are predicted to act as null expressors. γP121R and
εP121T shorten channel opening burst duration to 20% and
39% of corresponding wild-type AChR by increasing dissocia-
tion constant 6- and 7- fold and by reducing the channel gat-
ing equilibrium constant 34- and 24-fold, respectively.
Conclusions: We characterized two novel mutations at an
equivalent P121 residue in γ and ε subunits of AChR that
cause Escobar syndrome and a CMS, respectively. The
