tion of muscle fat fraction (MFF) in the lower extremities
with disease-speci ﬁc motor endpoints in patients with DM2.
Methods: Lower extremity 3T MRIs were obtained from
nine genetically proven patients with DM2 using the T1W-
Dixon volumetric interpolated breath-hold examination
(VIBE) protocol. Muscle volumes (15 mm thickness) were
segmented from the proximal, middle, and distal levels of the
thigh and calf compartments in both legs. MFF (%) was cal-
culated from the Dixon sequence by dividing the mean signal
intensity of the fat volumes by the summed mean signal
intensity of the fat and water volumes. Lower extremity MFF
was correlated with quantitative muscle strength and disease-
speciﬁc motor endpoints, including a six-minute walk dis-
tance (meter), 15-second step test, gait speed (second), and
grip strength (kg) using Spearman ’s rank correlation coef ﬁ-
cients (ρ). Bonferroni correction was used to adjust the num-
ber of comparisons; thus, the signi ﬁcant level was set at
α=0.05/4 or 0.0125.
Results: Mean age of the participants was 62 /C611 years
(89% female), and the mean disease duration was
20/C612 years. The highest MFF at the thigh was the posterior
compartment (39.7 /C612.9%), and the calf was the lateral
compartment (35.1 /C68.7%). The mean MFF of the posterior
thigh compartment was signi ﬁcantly correlated with a
6-minute walk distance ( ρ=- 0.90, P-value=0.001), while the
lateral calf MFF was strongly correlated with the 15-second
step test ( ρ=-0.820, P-value=0.006). Although not statistically
signiﬁcant, higher MFF in the posterior thigh compartment
was also moderately correlated with grip strength, gait speed,
and step test ( ρ=-0.61 to -0.73).
Conclusion: Our pilot data support the notion that quan-
titative measure of lower extremity MFF is strongly correlated
with motor performance in patients with DM2. A longitudi-
nal study and a matched control group are required to evalu-
ate lower extremity MFF as a biomarker of disease
progression and therapeutic responses in DM2.
