M224. Sporadic Burkitt Lymphoma Presenting with
Lumbar Spinal Canal Involvement in an Adult
Bahadar S. Srichawla, DO, MS . UMass Chan Medical
School, Worcester, MA, USA.
Burkitt lymphoma (BL) is an aggressive form of lymphoma that
occurs due to translocation of the MYC gene on chromosome
8. BL is characterized in three distinct groups: African/endemic
variant, immunosuppressive variant, or sporadic variant. Most
cases of the sporadic variant occur in patients less than 40 years
of age with a median age of 30 at diagnosis and are mainly seen
in Caucasians. Common presenting symptoms include an
abdominal mass that often affects the distal ileum or stomach.
An immunocompetent 69-year-old Hispanic American male
presented with subacute-onset lower extremity weakness. Neuro-
logic examination was signiﬁcant for bilateral hyperreﬂexia of the
lower extremities, ankle clonus, and 3/5 muscle strength. Mag-
netic resonance imaging (MRI) of the lumbar spine revealed a
right paraspinal mass with epidu ral extension leading to severe
spinal canal stenosis at L2/L3 and L4/L5. Given the signi ﬁcant
concern about a neoplastic process, the patient underwent com-
puted tomography (CT) scan of the chest which revealed bilat-
eral pulmonary nodules. The HIV test was negative.
Subsequently, the patient underwent a core needle biopsy
of the left axillary lymph node which revealed CD20,
CD10, c-MYC, and BCL6 positive lymphoid cells. The Ki-
67 proliferation index was greater than 90%. Fluorescence
in situ hybridization (FISH) s tudies showed the MYC::IGH
rearrangement while negative for the BCL2 and BCL6
rearrangement. A diagnosis of Burkitt lymphoma was made.
The patient was started on oral dexamethasone 4 mg every
6 hours with concern for spinal cord compression. And
received one round of radiation therapy. The patient was
considered a nonsurgical candidate. A goals of care discus-
sion was held, and the patient opted to forgo any antitumor
treatment and be discharged to hospice.
Here we report a unique case of sporadic Burkitt lymphoma
presenting with weakness of the lower extremities with primary
involvement of the right paraspinal tissue with epidural exten-
sion into the spinal canal. Primary lymphomas of the central
nervous system account for <5% of all CNS tumors. Approxi-
mately 5-10% of CNS lymphomas are recorded as Burkitt lym-
phomas, and the majority are classi ﬁed as high-grade B-cell
lymphomas. Paraspinal involvement with Burkitt lymphoma is
rare and is seen primarily in the endemic variant. Treatment
involves chemotherapy; common regimens include CODOX-M
with IVAC and dose-adjusted EPOCH.
