Dysregulated RNA alternative splicing (AS) is a hallmark of
cancer and contributes to the regulation of different processes
in cancers including glioma. However, whether the dys-
regulated AS and the regulating RNA-binding proteins (RBPs)
drive gliomagenesis and the role of glioma driver mutations in
shaping the RBP/AS landscape have not been comprehensively
studied. Here we describe two AS-de ﬁned glioma subtypes
that markedly correlate with IDH1 mutation, WHO tumor
grade, and patient prognosis. Oncogenic AS isoforms are
exclusively associated with AS subtype 2, IDH1 wild-type glio-
mas. Modulation of representative AS isoforms or subtype-
associated RBPs regulate glioma stem-like cell (GSC)
tumorgenicity. Utilizing a human induced pluripotent stem
cell-derived glioma avatar model system, we demonstrate that
speciﬁc glioma driver mutations are critical in shaping the
subtype-associated AS programs. Lastly, targeting a
sub2-associated RBP with anti-sense oligonucleotides inhibited
growth of GSC tumor xenografts in the brain of animals. Our
data establish relationships between glioma driver mutations
and AS programs and identify dysregulated RBP/AS network
as a therapeutic vulnerability.
