3 and/or 6 months later. Subjects also completed the Neuro-
behavioral Symptom Inventory (NSI) for evaluation of their
neurological symptoms. For the initial testing session ( n=77),
a signi ﬁcant proportion of subjects (up to 86%) scored out-
side established normative ranges for one or more tests, with
smooth pursuit and optokinetic responses being most severely
affected. A multivariate model constructed using stepwise
logistic regression identi ﬁed 6 metrics as signi ﬁcant predictors
of post-COVID group membership (speci ﬁcity=98%, sensi-
tivity=88%). In line with the prolonged duration of the
underlying condition (long-COVID), the impairments rev-
ealed by our testing persisted in multiple subjects tested
6 months later (n=19), although some improvement was
seen. Comparing the initial versus the 6-month assessments,
for instance, 10/19 subjects initially had impaired vertical
smooth pursuit tracking, and of those, 6 recovered to perfor-
mance that was within normative limits (60%). By contrast,
of the 9/19 subjects with impaired high-speed optokinetic
nystagmus gain, only one recovered to normative
